  "query_preview": "..Current radar analysis shows light showers with\nembedded thunder across central and northern Utah with more robust\nconvection across southern Utah that has been producing\n0.25-0.5\"/hour. Additionally, southerly winds are picking up ahead\nof an approaching trough. Gusts in the 40s and a few isolated 50s\nare noted across most of western Utah. These winds will continue\nthrough the evening and into tomorrow as remnants of tropical\nstorm Rachel move into the area.\n\nRachel is currently moving northwest toward the northern Baja\nCalifornia with an anticipated landfall overnight tonight. NHC has\nnoted that Rachel has been tracking to the right of the forecast\ntrack which has shift the forecast track slightly more towards the\neast compared to yesterday when the center of circulation was\nexpected to move through southern Nevada. Now that track is more\nlikely moving through southwestern Utah. However, guidance still\ncontinues to struggle with the exact track, but regardless the\nimpacts will primarily remain the same: widespread rain, heavy at\ntimes, strong southerly winds, and the potential for strong to\nsevere thunderstorms.\n\nThe most widespread activity today will be across eastern Utah\nwith moderate to heavy rainfall rates possible along with frequent\nlightning. The more widespread precipitation move in tonight\nthrough tomorrow with a moderate risk of flash flooding across\nsouthwestern Utah where upslope flow along southerly facing\nterrain will enhance precipitation totals. Areas along northerly\nfacing aspects that tend to do poorly in southerly flow could be\nshadowed a bit, such as the northern half of the Salt Lake Valley\nand Cedar City. This has been evident in the CAMs, but the globals\nstruggle depicting these smaller scale features. Additionally, a\nfavorable shear signature and strong synoptic flow will help fuel\nsome strong to severe thunderstorms, primarily across the \nsouthern half and eastern half of Utah. These will be capable of \nstrong gusty winds, an increased risk of flash flooding due to \nhigher rainfall rates, and a tornado or two.\n\nOverall precipitation will be between 1-3 inches with locally\nhigher amounts of 4\"+ across the favored upslope regions. This\namount is just through early Monday morning. Areas of central and\nnorthern Utah may actually see more precipitation with the closed\nlow that slowly moves through Monday-Wednesday. This will bring\ncolder air aloft with snow levels falling to ~7500 feet by\nWednesday along with 6-12 inches of snow above 9000 feet with\nsome locations that are favored in northwest flow receiving over\n15\". Some ensemble members are showing upwards of 30\" of snow at\nthe top of the Cottonwoods which might be a bit aggressive, but a\nstout northwest flow wrapping around this closed low and a warm\nlake that could lead to some lake effect could make for an\noverperforming event.",
